WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.520
Tiers characterize the rigor of cybersecurity risk governance and management practices.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:08.420
They help describe whether practices are less formal and reactive,

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.640
more repeatable and risk-informed, or more adaptive over time.

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:16.860
A Tier is not a simple grade, trophy, or vanity maturity label.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.240
It should be interpreted in context.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:24.180
A small organization and a highly regulated enterprise may have different needs,

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:27.680
resources, risk tolerance, and target rigor.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.780
Tiers can help communication when they are used carefully.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:36.680
They can explain why governance, ownership, evidence, or repeatability needs to improve.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:41.780
They can also help leaders understand that cybersecurity maturity is not only about buying

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:42.800
tools.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:47.740
It is about risk management practices, decision quality, repeatable processes,

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:49.720
and continuous improvement.

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:54.740
The danger is treating a Tier as a slogan instead of a useful description of how work is

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.320
actually managed.

00:00:56.540 --> 00:01:01.500
Informative References connect CSF outcomes to other standards, control catalogs,

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:03.960
frameworks, and guidance.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:08.740
They help teams translate outcome language into more specific sources when planning

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:10.080
implementation.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:14.540
Implementation Examples provide possible ways that outcomes may be achieved.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:17.720
They are examples, not universal requirements.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:22.980
A team should still decide what fits its architecture, risk, policy, and operating model.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:29.280
CSF 2.0 also has supporting resources such as Quick Start Guides, Community Profiles,

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:32.060
online tools, and reference material.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:37.400
These resources can help an organization start small, scope work, compare outcomes,

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:39.140
and communicate plans.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:44.560
The key is to use them as aids for practical adoption, not as substitutes for ownership,

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:47.340
evidence, and risk-based decision-making.
